        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the Toronto market",
        "market_text": "<p>Toronto is one of the most condo-dense markets in North America, and an empty 500 sq ft unit in a King West tower photographs like every other empty unit in the building. Home staging is how a listing separates itself when a buyer is flipping through forty near-identical floor plans.</p><p>Small-space proof is the specific job here. In a market where a large share of inventory is one-bedroom and junior one-bedroom units, the buyer's real question is whether a bed, a sofa and a work-from-home setup can coexist. Staged to scale, the photo answers it; empty, the buyer assumes the answer is no.</p><p>Toronto's freehold market has the opposite problem. Narrow Victorian semis and century homes in the older neighbourhoods have room proportions and additions that empty photography makes confusing, and buyers at those price points are unforgiving about listings that don't present well.</p>",
